{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-155515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-155515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}-1"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}-2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{2}^{x}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(3)={\\int }_{2}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+{3}^{2})-({2}^{3}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(27+9)-(8+4) \\\\  & =36-12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "35"] ["math" "39"] ["math" "47"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-{e}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-1"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-51217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4}-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-346529" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346529" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(({3}^{3})^{1/2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10+\\pi "] ["math" "10-\\pi "] ["math" "6-\\pi "] ["math" "8+\\pi "] ["math" "8-\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-103770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10-\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/21c86a5542f6bac4cd53c9d64d7c24a90ea6e901bd58e0204f38a91c2835e3aa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/b14fbfe336923b44e4e4f3660bef7c6be97ada82ea07c4523a3f25f2905f4fd6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,3],$  for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square.  This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+9=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{27}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +16"] ["math" "2\\pi +4"] ["math" "2\\pi +8"] ["math" "\\pi +4"] ["math" "\\pi +8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-156193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/d41d681a2f0e7a32400d863e60294efc9864ff6245de0e36515986da656ddbd2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=5,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/7db30a3be837d9303ce4fca0b5a95466a74fe53f1ce81e6fa0febe2ef00625b5.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,5],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,5],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+(12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi ) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15-\\frac{9}{2}\\pi "] ["math" "16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi "] ["math" "17-\\frac{3}{2}\\pi "] ["math" "18-\\frac{3}{2}\\pi "] ["math" "18-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-155948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total mass, in kilograms, of compost produced $t$ days after a process begins increases at a rate of $7{e}^{t}.$ If, at $t=0$ days, the process had already produced $5$ kilograms of compost, how much compost will the process have produced after $2$ days?\n\nAfter $2$ days, the process has produced\n{{field-1}} kilograms of compost." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $a(t)=7{e}^{t}$ be the rate at which the amount of compost produced is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of compost produced $T$ days after the process begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{T})-(7{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5+7{e}^{T}-7 \\\\  & =7{e}^{T}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of compost produced $T=2$ days after the process begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=5+7{e}^{2}-7=7{e}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ days, the process has produced $7{e}^{2}-2$ kilograms of compost." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-346579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7e^{2}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "52"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fuel is being pumped into a storage tank at a rate of $8t$ gallons per hour. If at time $t=0$ the tank already contains $12$ gallons of fuel, write an expression for $V(t),$ the total amount of fuel in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t)=8t$ represent the rate at which fuel enters the tank, and let $V(T)$ be the total amount of fuel in the tank after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nV(T)=V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(T) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{0}^{T}8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={12+(4{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =12+4{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nV(t)=12+4{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-346569" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346569" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12+4t^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An engineer started tracking the amount of electric charge stored in a battery. The initial charge was $75$ coulombs, and the charging and discharging rates are given by $6+{t}^{3/2}$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of hours since the beginning of the observation.  \n\nFind an expression for the charge $C(t)$ stored in the battery at any time $t>0.$\n\n$C(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $c(t)$ be the rate of change of the charge. According to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)={{6+{t}^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge decrease}}=5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ represent the charge stored $T$ hours after $2015.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+(5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =75+5T+\\frac{2}{5}{T}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nC(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-346582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-346582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5t+\\frac{2}{5}t^{5/2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A pumping station adds sand to Sandy Beach at a rate of $3\\sqrt{t}$ cubic yards per hour. The tide removes the sand from the beach at a rate of $2t$ cubic yards per hour. If at time $t=0$ the beach contains $1\\,500$ cubic yards of sand, write an expression for $A(t),$ the total number of cubic yards of sand on the beach at any time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $a(t)=3\\sqrt{t}-2t$ be the rate at which the volume of sand at Sandy Beach is changing. Note that we subtract $2t$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $A(T)$ be the volume of sand on the beach at any time after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3\\sqrt{t}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{(2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =1\\,500+2{T}^{3/2}-{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have \n\n$$\nA(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A(t)=2{t}^{3/2}+{t}^{2}+1\\,500"] ["math" "A(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500"] ["math" "A(t)=3{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500"] ["math" "A(t)=\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}+{t}^{2}+1\\,500"] ["math" "A(t)={t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "q-2370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fc9e24bf0046ace2f0bc42bcd892e3bfe351861c83dfd74064cdfff2b584571"} {"question" "e-10876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10876.json" "file_sha256" "80d57b28d1dd12653b9b55554ed98c3e1faad441e5e75b8a638b0fb308d9f19d"} {"question" "e-10876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi ) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10876.json" "file_sha256" "80d57b28d1dd12653b9b55554ed98c3e1faad441e5e75b8a638b0fb308d9f19d"} {"question" "e-10884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10884.json" "file_sha256" "2c881db5a41de7139dd80e4adb90b674ed65b410f23c65423e44b2d038a0957c"} {"question" "e-10884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10884.json" "file_sha256" "2c881db5a41de7139dd80e4adb90b674ed65b410f23c65423e44b2d038a0957c"} {"question" "e-10877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a pool has a small hole and that water is leaking out at a rate of $t^{1/3}$ gallons per minute, where $t$ is the time in minutes. If at time $t = 0$ the pool contains $100$ gallons of water, how many gallons of water will remain in the pool after $t$ minutes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10877.json" "file_sha256" "639adaf90c0483255a26bda71870b0a565f2ec797461e44d9f54c7fca0627fe4"} {"question" "e-10877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t) = -t^{1/3}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) \t\t\t= F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-{\\int }_{0}^{T}{t}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3{t}^{4/3}}{4}{|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3(T)^{4/3}}{4}+\\frac{3(0)^{4/3}}{4} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{4/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/example-10877.json" "file_sha256" "639adaf90c0483255a26bda71870b0a565f2ec797461e44d9f54c7fca0627fe4"}] "usage" {"e-10876" [] "q-2370" [] "q-155717" [] "q-155515" [] "e-10884" [] "q-155948" [] "q-155514" [] "e-10877" []} "basis" 1147 "retractions" [[17592186064430 :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\frac{π}{2})$ where $F(x) = ∫_{π/4}^{x}2\\cos tdt$."] [17592186064429 :question/problem "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x) = ∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}dt$."] [17592186064431 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/q-155717.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t) = ∫_{-3}^{t}f(x)dx$, determine the value of $g(3)$."] [17592186064432 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/q-155948.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $3$. Given $g(t) = ∫_{0}^{t}f(x)dx$, determine the value of $g(5)$."] [17592186064547 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA pumping station adds sand to Sandy Beach at a rate of $3\\sqrt{t}$ cubic yards per hour. The tide removes the sand from the beach at a rate of $2t$ cubic yards per hour. If at time $t = 0$ the beach contains $1500$ cubic yards of sand, write an expression for $A(t)$, the total number of cubic yards of sand on the beach at any time $t > 0$."] [17592186063840 :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t$."] [17592186063840 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(π) &= ∫_{1}^{π}\\frac{1}{t}dt \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid t \\mid \\mid _{1}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\ln π - \\ln 1 \\\\\n&= \\ln π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063842 :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x$, determine the value of $g(3)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/6a389b5bb31f7ee2ea2b8194d93fdc6d.png)"] [17592186063842 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/0c342ebdf2da41b0cc85cdb1342abbcd.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1$.\n- $x \\in [0,3]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2$.\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0]$, we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2 + 3); = 5$\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3]$, we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot (2 + 4); = 9$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(3) &= ∫_{-2}^{3}f(x)dx = A_{1} + A_{2} = 5 + 9 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063841 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(t) = -t^{1/3}\n$$\n\nbe the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) = F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(T) &= F(0) + ∫_{0}^{T}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= 100 - ∫_{0}^{T}t^{1/3}dt \\\\\n&= 100 - \\frac{3t^{4/3}}{4} \\mid _{0}^{T} \\\\\n&= 100 - (3(T)^{4/3})/(4) + (3(0)^{4/3})/(4) \\\\\n&= 100 - \\frac{3}{4}T^{4/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t$. We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186061259 17592186061260 17592186061261 17592186061262 17592186061263 17592186061265 17592186061266 17592186061267 17592186061268 17592186061269 17592186061271 17592186061272 17592186061273 17592186061274 17592186061275 17592186061277 17592186061278 17592186061279 17592186061280 17592186061281 17592186061794 17592186061795 17592186061796 17592186061797 17592186061798] "immutable_fields" [[17592186061258 :answer-field/correct] [17592186061258 :answer-field/id] [17592186061258 :answer-field/key] [17592186